  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}=7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =7\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x-2}+4x\\cdot {6}^{x-2}-7\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+4x-7)\\cdot {6}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+4x-7=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+7)(x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{7}{3},1,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{7}{3})\\cdot 1=-\\frac{7}{3}.$",
